### O.V.S.Ch (Global Open Synchronous Championship), tour 2 (ОВСЧ–2)

# Edited by Eugene Mirotin [me-RAW-tin].

# Tested by Anastasia Rozhina [RAW-zhee-nah], Ksenia Repson-Deforge, Yuri Razumov [RAH-zoo-mov], Andrey Zabavin [za-BAH-vin], Alexandr Matyukhin [ma-TOO-khin], Alexey Shtykh, Anna Svirina [SVEE-ree-nah], Aleksandr Pecheny [pe-CHAW-ny], Nikita Vorobyov [voh-rah-BYOV], Daniil Fedkevich [fed-KEH-vich], Alexandr Karchevsky [kar-CHEV-sky], team “Vihasoo–21”, Fedor Zhelne [zhel-NEH], Alexey Boronenko [boh-raw-NEN-ko].

# In any questions, X and Y can be used to substitute words, in singular or plural, concepts or phrases. Words in square brackets indicate phonetic transcription.

## Block 1

? In the 1960s, the American Civil Liberties Union fought for interracial marriage rights. They selected a trial between the married couple Richard and Mildred Loving and the state of Virginia for their public campaign. Historian Jill Lepore [le-PORE] writes that this choice was driven by… what?
! the [sur]name Loving [accept answers with “name”, “surname”, “last name” without incorrect information].
/ historian and journalist Jill Lepore writes that the ACLU wanted the Loving v. Virginia case to be about love.
^ https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2021/11/happy-elephant-bronx-zoo-nhrp-lawsuit/620672/
@ Eugene Mirotin

? When the protagonist of the Kalevala lost his musical instrument, he made a new one out of wood. In this process, he was aided by a maiden, who donated… what?
! her hair [accept any answers that mention hair].
/ Väinämöinen played the kantele, a traditional stringed instrument. The maiden gave him her hair to make the strings.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kantele
@ Eugene Mirotin

? People who live close to Mount Etna do not perceive the recurring sound of the volcano as frightening. On the contrary, it makes them feel like their beloved is close to them. What do they, facetiously, compare this sound to?
! snoring [accept [a] snore].
/ “When Mount Etna rumbles, it’s like a snore of somebody you love. It reminds you that they are close.” Indeed, volcanoes can be dormant, but Etna is an active one.
^ Wizz Air magazine, June–July 2022 // https://lxm-group.aflip.in/wizz-europe-June-July-2022.html#page/38
@ Eugene Mirotin

? In certain languages, the script is written vertically instead of horizontally. Sofia Valitova [vah-LEE-tow-vah] compares the issue of underlining text in such languages to another conundrum concerning X and Y. What is X and what are Y?
! a dog, pants [accept trousers/jeans instead of pants].
/ the question is whether the column should be scored vertically, or each character should be underscored individually. It reminded Sofia of a popular joke question:
if a dog wore pants, would it wear them on all four legs, or only on the two rear legs?
(img 2022-2023_II_en_001.png)
^ https://twitter.com/ariarzer/status/1561746093007945728
@ Eugene Mirotin

? The isle of Corfu has a curved and elongated shape. Legend has it that the isle was created in ancient times from an object used and thrown away by this figure. At a later time, due to the similarity of their names, this figure became identified… with what other figure?
! [with] Chronos.
/ according to one of the legends, Corfu was created when Cronus threw away the sickle with which he castrated his father Uranus. Initially Cronus, the god of agriculture, and Chronos, the god of time, were two different deities, but later they merged due to the similarity of their names. In order to hint at Chronos, we used the word “time” twice.
^ 
- https://www.panasadventures.co.uk/one-famous-island-with-many-namesone-famous-island-with-many-names
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corfu
- https://www.timetravelrome.com/2020/09/06/korkyra-a-sanctuary-of-the-mythological/
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Кронос
@ Eugene Mirotin

? According to one hypothesis, an island near the coast of Corfu was named after the church of the Holy Virgin of Nazareth. Another hypothesis suggests that the name appeared during Venetian rule. What did the Venetians build on that island?
! a leper house [accept lazaret[to], hospital].
/ the main etymological hypothesis is that the isle of Lazareto was named by the Venetians who built a leper house there and named the island similarly to the Venetian isle of Lazaretto, where the knights of the Order of Saint Lazarus built a leper house in the 14th century. According to another hypothesis, the Greek name is the distorted word Nazareth, and it appeared long before the Venetians.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Лазарето
- https://el.wikipedia.org/wiki/Λαζαρέτο_Κέρκυρας
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Лазаретто
@ Eugene Mirotin

? Florian Illies [EEL-lee-yes] notes that one of the participants of an ideological feud was significantly younger than the other, and calls the conflict a “patricide”. Name both of the participants.
! [Sigmund] Freud, [Carl Gustav] Jung.
/ patricide is a part of the Oedipus myth, which was very important for Freud. Indeed, Jung was almost 20 years younger than his teacher, and his name literally translates as “young”. At a certain point, the teacher and the student diverged in their views on psychoanalysis.
^ Florian Illies. 1913: The Year Before The Storm [Иллиес, Флориан. 1913. Лето целого века. — М.: Ад Маргинем Пресс: Музей современного искусства «Гараж», 2022. С. 27.]
@ Dmitry Goldov

? The assignment operator in one programming language consists of the colon and the equals sign. The mascot of the language is a gopher, which is why the assignment operator was nicknamed after X. What famous principle mentions X?
! lex talionis [accept talion principle; [an] eye for an eye, [a tooth for a tooth]].
/ the “colon and equals sign” combination looks like an emoticon, which is why Go programmers nicknamed it “the bucktooth operator”. Buckteeth are the protruding front teeth like those of a gopher. The colon ostensibly corresponds to the eyes. Both eyes and teeth are mentioned in the ancient principle of equal retaliation.
^ 
- https://twitter.com/val_deleplace/status/1354860411020042245?s=20
- https://changelog.com/gotime/168
@ Eugene Mirotin

? [When reading the question out loud, do not disclose the quotation marks; if displaying the question on screen, the quotation marks must be removed.]
In the mid-1980s, Beate Klarsfeld undertook several ‘hunting’ trips to South America, for which she received the Margaret Thatcher award. What name did we substitute in this question?
! Golda Meir.
/ Beate’s trips were actually unrelated to England or the Falklands War. Klarsfeld was one of the so-called Nazi hunters who tried to make sure that none of the war criminals in hiding avoid their punishment. We substituted one woman prime minister for another.
^ https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Кларсфельд,_Беата
@ Andrey Akimov (Minsk)

? Campari liqueur was invented in Piedmont. The cocktail in which Campari is united with orange juice is named after HIM. What is HIS surname?
! Garibaldi.
/ Piedmont is in the north of Italy, and oranges grow in the south, for example, in Sicily. The cocktail combining these two ingredients was named after the person who did the most to unite Italy — Giuseppe Garibaldi. By the way, the first capital of the unified Italy was Turin, the most prominent city of Piedmont.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campari
- https://www.liquor.com/recipes/garibaldi/
@ Alexandr Matyukhin

? Handout:
.zi...
A famous cartoon was so popular in the US that it returned to movie theaters several times. In 1959, the year of another relaunch, the Republican president Dwight Eisenhower received zi [zee] as a present. Write the four letters that we omitted in this question.
! Dmbo.
/ the mascot of the Republican party is an elephant, so Eisenhower was gifted a baby elephant named Dzimbo. We omitted the letters of his name shared with the name of the cartoon elephant Dumbo. The 1941 cartoon was so popular that it was shown in movie theaters again in 1949, 1959, 1972 and 1976, which is reminiscent of circus artists returning to the arena for the encore.
^ 
- https://www.whitehousehistory.org/photos/photo-4-10
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dumbo
@ Eugene Mirotin

? In a speech before a female audience, one Republican senator opined that an elephant is a great symbol, because it has X in the front and Y in the back. Name X.
! a vacuum cleaner.
/ the senator was not too smart, so he considered it appropriate to remind politicized women that their actual vocation is house work. He compared the trunk to a vacuum cleaner, and the tail to a rug beater, but if you thought of a broom, that’s fine too.
^ https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2021/11/happy-elephant-bronx-zoo-nhrp-lawsuit/620672/
@ Eugene Mirotin

## Block 2

? Dolly Oesterreich secretly lodged her lover in her attic. The media gave her lover the same moniker that another person has been using since the late 1930s. What moniker?
! Batman.
/ in 1918, Oesterreich forced her husband to move to a house with an attic in which she put up her lover. On one occasion, when the married couple were having an argument, the lover descended from the attic and shot the husband. Both lovers miraculously escaped imprisonment, and after the story became publicized, American media nicknamed the lover Batman because he lived in the attic for 10 years like a bat. Bruce Wayne has been using the nickname Batman ever since his first appearance in a 1939 comic book.
^ 
- https://bigpicture.ru/strannaya-istoriya-valburgi-osterrajx-kotoraya-godami-derzhala-tajnogo-lyubovnika-na-cherdake/
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Бэтмен
@ Andrey Akimov (Minsk)

? A cartoon character tries to chat to a guy she likes, but during every conversation he is distracted by some sloppy detail in her looks. For example, he notes that the character has something stuck in her teeth. Who is this character?
! [a] vampire.
/ the character cannot notice any sloppy details because she is unable to see herself in the mirror. The mention of teeth is a slight hint. With this question, we continued the bat theme.
^ Hotel Transylvania: The Series
@ Eugene Mirotin

? Comic book character Kingpin wears body armour under his clothes. The actor who played Kingpin in several TV series starred… in what famous 1987 movie?
! Full Metal Jacket.
/ the title of the movie in which Vincent D’Onofrio played Gomer Pyle can be taken to mean ‘metal body armour’, even though it actually means ‘bullet jacket’. To be completely honest, we have to add that Kingpin’s armour is also made of kevlar and not metal.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingpin_(character)#Skills,_abilities
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Д’Онофрио,_Винсент
- https://marvelcinematicuniverse.fandom.com/wiki/Kingpin
@ Fedor Zhelne

? Norwegian company Glassvatt manufactured fiberglass. To advertise its product, the owner of the company organized a car rally from Norway to Africa. What was loaded in the car at the start of the rally?
! ice [accept snow].
/ fibreglass is an insulant, i.e. a thermal retainer. To prove the quality of its product, the company owner sent a truck with three tons of ice from Norway to Africa. It was an outstanding success: only 11% of the cargo was lost on the way to Gabon.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_block_expedition_of_1959
@ Eugene Mirotin

? The ice delivery expedition to Africa proved a success, and its participants became famous. A small amount of the ice was returned to Norway, and at the premiere of a movie about the expedition, it ended up… where?
! in drinks [accept: in [glasses with] drinks/cocktails, in champagne buckets]
/ the expedition organizer did not miss his chance to promote it even more. Some of the ice was brought back, cut into cubes, and served with drinks to the journalists invited to the premiere.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_block_expedition_of_1959
@ Eugene Mirotin

? One traditional drinking competition is held in spring in the open air. It is believed that if the pollen of X falls into Y, the drinker will gain greater power. Name X and Y with words that begin with the same few letters.
! sakura, sake.
/ during the hanami, the blooming sakura appreciation days, the Japanese also hold “who-drinks-the-most-and-can-still-stand-straight” contests. The picnics take place right under the trees, and some sakura pollen might sometimes fall into the cups, which is supposed to make the drinkers stronger and healthier.
^ 
- https://en.sake-times.com/learn/sake-and-sakura
- https://www.snakku.com/blogs/news/113343109-hanami-observing-the-brief-eternity-of-cherry-blossoms
- https://enjourney.ru/obzori/o_hanami_cvetenie_sakuri_89/otlichniy_povod_otdohnut_ot_raboti_273
- https://aminoapps.com/c/igryanimerisova/page/item/top-5-faktov-o-sakure/qPpY_aRF3Inma4qzx5kzMN4dpnRL0GJnov
@ Andrey Akimov (Minsk)

? For added flavour, this thing might be infused with lemon balm, thyme or mint. One website compares this thing to a time capsule. Name this thing using two words.
! dandelion wine.
/ dandelion wine is a real low-alcohol beverage popular in England. Many other herbs and plants are often added to it. In Ray Bradbury’s novel, dandelion wine is called “summer caught and stoppered”, and its flavor and scent brings the summer back.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Вино_из_одуванчиков_(алкогольный_напиток)
- https://www.etsy.com/listing/129557625/dandelion-wine-box-custom-wine-box
@ Andrey Akimov (Minsk)

? In South Korea, the gwarosa [kwa-RAW-sah] also presents a problem, so the government even issued a decree lowering the standard from 68 to 52. 52 of what?
! [work]hours [per week].
/ in Japan, there is the concept of karoshi — death by fatigue caused by overwork. Such death is considered honorable, and similar phenomena are also observed in other Asian countries. We are more accustomed to 40 hour workweeks.
^ https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Кароси#Южная_Корея
