## Block 3

? Handout:
There are two things in life for which we are never truly prepared: things.
Characters of a TV series are surprised to find out that the fingerprints of the person they detained do not match the fingerprints at the crime scene. The episode ends with the quote in which we have changed one word. What was the original word?
! twins.
/ the characters find out that they detained the criminal’s twin brother. The episode ends with an aphorism by the writer Josh Billings: “There are two things in life for which we are never truly prepared: twins”.
^ «Criminal Minds», S9E1
@ Andrey Akimov (Minsk), idea by Vladimir Ulyanitsky (Minsk)

? In a poem, Boris Pasternak compares a grand piano on the top floor of a high rise with this object. Which one of these objects was depicted in the original logo of a well-known radio station?
! [the] Liberty Bell.
/ the grand piano in the high rise is compared to a bell in the bell tower. The original logo of Radio Liberty depicted the Liberty Bell, a symbol of the US independence. In 2005, though, the logo was changed to a torch.
^ 
- Boris Pasternak, Music // https://www.ruthenia.ru/60s/pasternak/kogda/music.htm
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Радио_«Свобода»
@ Eugene Mirotin

? When Greeks ask somebody to calm down, they might ask them to perform this action. The Greeks thought that only a god was able to forgo this action and not give in to madness. Deduce the god’s identity and describe this action.
! to dilute wine with water.
/ in Ancient Greece, it was customary to drink diluted wine — it was considered that only Bacchus can drink the pure stuff without going on a rampage. The modern phrase, meaning “to calm down in an argument”, refers to that tradition.
^ https://oenouyi.wine/pour-some-water-in-your-wine-why-did-ancient-greeks-dilute-their-wine-and-what-does-the-expression-mean/
@ Eugene Mirotin

? According to Greek popular wisdom, it is particularly important to avoid drinking undiluted wine in hot weather. The Greeks use a simple heuristic analogous to another principle regarding THEM used in a different country. Name THEM.
! oysters.
/ “If there is no letter R in the name of the month, wine should be diluted with water”. In France, it is traditionally considered that one should not eat oysters in months without the letter R. One of the explanations is that the reproductive period of the molluscs lasts from May to August, and a liquid is formed in their shells that makes their flesh bitter.
^ 
- https://ru.wikiquote.org/wiki/Греческие_пословицы
- https://vogue.ua/article/culture/restorany/ustricy-gid-ot-a-do-ya.html#:~:text=«Р».,молоко, придающее мясу горький вкус.
@ Eugene Mirotin

? In one production of Faust, Mephistopheles asks Bacchus for some wine. After that, a statue nearby starts… doing what?
! bleeding [accept all synonymous answers with mention of blood].
/ the devil commits blasphemy, and a Christian statue nearby starts bleeding. The director probably refers to the fact that in Christian culture, wine is called the blood of Jesus, and to miracles involving bleeding statues of saints. Other Christian statues are believed to cry or to secrete myrrh.
^ production of Faust in Royal Opera House, London, watched at medici.tv
@ Eugene Mirotin

? Beethoven called himself a free thinker. In his Eroica, he reused the music from his own ballet dedicated to... what figure?
! Prometheus.
/ Prometheus is perhaps the foremost free thinker in Greek mythology. The fact that Beethoven wrote a ballet is also curious in itself.
^ https://arzamas.academy/micro/symph/3
@ Eugene Mirotin

? Writer Richard Thomas described a literary trope in which the author interrupts a story to express their own position or opinion. He named this trope after a collective of people. Name this collective with one word.
! chorus [accept Greek chorus].
/ similarly, the choir in Greek theater used to comment on certain events, thoughts and actions of characters. Thomas is not the only one to use this term with regard to the author’s voice in modern prose, but one cannot say it’s an established term either.
^ https://litreactor.com/columns/storyville-using-a-chorus-in-your-fiction
@ Eugene Mirotin

? Andrew Doyle’s book Free Speech is dedicated to so-called freedom of speech. On the cover, four letters in the book’s title are substituted by X. What are X?
! quotation marks [accept quotes, speech marks, double quotes, inverted commas].
/ quotation marks are used to identify direct speech, and they may also be synonymous with the phrase “so-called”. In the cover, the pairs of “e”’s in each word are substituted for double quotes. Whether free speech actually exists is a subject for a whole treatise.
^ Andrew Doyle, Free Speech And Why It Matters
@ Eugene Mirotin

? Early in his career, the artist James Whistler was constantly attacked by critics. Oscar Wilde quipped that X is “the only insult that has not yet been offered to Mr. Whistler”. Later, however, the painter was bestowed with an order of merit and elected as an honorary member of the Academy of Fine Arts. X gave the name to an art movement. What movement?
! pop art.
/ at first, James Whistler was not very well known. He long strove for acknowledgement as an artist but was constantly criticized for his style, so Wilde even joked that popularity is the only insult that has not yet been offered to Whistler. Later, though, Whistler earned recognition and became famous in the West, and his Mother is one of the world’s most recognizable paintings that has been commonly referenced in popular culture. Pop art is an art movement whose representatives include Andy Warhol.
^ 
- https://ru.bookmate.com/books/ybTOB6AK/quotes
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Уистлер,_Джеймс
@ Fedor Zhelne

? A uniform with two unusual metal buttons was developed for the Royal Air Force in the 1940s. One of these buttons was convex, and the other had a special mark on the edge. In case of emergency, the pilot could tear the buttons off and use them as… what?
! a compass.
/ the pilot would put the button with the marked polarized edge onto the convex button, so it could rotate. This way, a pilot could assemble a makeshift compass and find his way in an unfamiliar landscape after a plane crash. Brilliant idea — the only inconvenience was that the buttons would have been ripped off from the fly of the trousers.
^ https://www.orau.org/health-physics-museum/collection/radioluminescent/wwii-raf-button-compass.html
@ Eugene Mirotin

? In the Second World War, a German plane crashed on the isle of Prangli. The survivor pilot had to pay to be transported to the continent. The local women ended up very happy. What did the pilot pay with?
! a parachute.
/ parachutes were made out of silk, so the women got a lot of expensive cloth which they used to make dresses.
^ 
- Story told by a tour guide at the isle of Prangli
- http://battal.ee/prangli/prangli04.html
@ Irina Kolesnikova

? Hans Christian Andersen was a son of a shoemaker. To describe the method he used in editing his texts, the author of the question used two verbs. Write the three-letter one of them.
! cut.
/ when editing his texts, Andersen literally cut fragments out with scissors and sewed them into the right place, even though he could use shoe glue just as well. In any case, it was a kind of cut-and-paste method akin to modern digital editing. The word “Cut!” also means the end of a take, and this question concludes our tournament.
^ Konstantin Paustovsky. The Great Taleteller [К. Паустовский, Великий сказочник]
@ Eugene Mirotin

